  "scope": [
    "Native Linux x64, pinned Bun 1.4.2 Chrome backend and stock headless shell 148.0.7778.96.",
    "Compare native closeAll with documented view.cdp Browser.close, both with and without a confirmed pending evaluation in a second view.",
    "Four initial controls followed by eight reversed-order controls with a five-second bounded wait for original browser PID/start identity disappearance.",
    "Same experimental namespace/cgroup collector: wrapper and probe inside a fresh leaf, collector outside. Original terminal wait is for bwrap, not Bun.",
    "Private temporary mount preserves inherited display sockets. No application suites, workload timing samples or comparative performance claims."
  ],
  "findings": [
    "Raw Browser.close resolves in every tested CDP case. Pending evaluate rejects and later evaluate on the closed view rejects.",
    "The first CDP control left one browser-root process for the collector to reap. After adding bounded original-root disappearance observation, none of the eight later controls left an adopted browser root. Kernel identity disappearance is not a native Bun wait receipt.",
    "Every case still caused descendant adoption and failed the current collector clean-custody policy. Some captures also required collector termination of surviving processes.",
    "Raw Browser.close is an available lifecycle primitive, but neither its response nor view closure proves complete process drain or CPU ownership. The existing native benchmark adapter remains unchanged."
  ],
